{"parentUuid":"71c12b25-d849-4758-93c3-ab0787c93472","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af150891e26339789","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01WtGPJMHCrfMKnrM4XhbqQX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LtWzjRAP5TigAWyDNhGZYa","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"hybrid CP MIP decomposition CP-SAT Gurobi binding subproblem scheduling"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6018,"cache_read_input_tokens":21242,"output_tokens":473,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6018},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":473,"cache_read_input_tokens":21242,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6018,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6018,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoraZRTskgN3tqbU3j3z","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9a6d8c8d-b292-48c6-9076-2c3443f3fc93","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:40.693Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3fd37b06-1975-4c36-a624-f6d8fce7e15b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F469asxEDkqzQ6nkPZa18W","type":"tool_result","content":"# Multi-Level Logic-Based Benders Decomposition for Hierarchical Rectangle Packing\n\nBased on the available abstract, I can provide limited details about this method, as the full paper content isn't included in the source material.\n\n## What We Know\n\nThe paper proposes \"a novel multilevel Logic-based Benders Decomposition method\" that represents a key contribution to solving hierarchical rectangle packing problems. This approach is designed to handle the recursive structure where items can be either individual rectangles or block occurrences whose dimensions come from solving nested packing subproblems.\n\n## Key Distinguishing Feature\n\nThe method's primary advantage is that it \"dynamically refines block dimension constraints, eliminating the need for manual selection of candidate widths or aspect ratios.\" This automated refinement process appears central to the approach's effectiveness.\n\n## Performance Results\n\nThe experimental evaluation demonstrates that \"the proposed decomposition significantly outperforms both monolithic formulations and the Bottom-Up method in terms of solution quality and scalability,\" particularly on synthetic instances with up to seven hierarchy levels and 80 items per block under limited computation time.\n\n## Limitation\n\nUnfortunately, the provided source material (an arXiv abstract page) doesn't contain sufficient detail to explain the master/sub problem structure, specific level organization strategy, or solver implementation specifics you're asking about. These details would require access to the full paper."}]},"uuid":"eeeb3ce4-8938-460e-990d-e7860da45470","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:44.896Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3fd37b06-1975-4c36-a624-f6d8fce7e15b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"71c12b25-d849-4758-93c3-ab0787c93472","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RcF6H5ZhTvv2CGfMMps3YZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"geographic spatial decomposition grid placement subregion 2D layout exact\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Geographic Information Technology Training Alliance (GITTA) presents:\",\"url\":\"http://www.gitta.info/SpatPartitio/en/text/SpatPartitio.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Quadtree - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadtree\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) 2D Map Alignment With Region Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/319463865_2D_Map_Alignment_With_Region_Decomposition\"},{\"title\":\"Spatial Index: Grid Systems | PYBLOG\",\"url\":\"https://www.pyblog.xyz/spatial-index-grid-system\"},{\"title\":\"Spatial Subdivision - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/spatial-subdivision\"},{\"title\":\"Data-Spatial Layouts for Grid Maps Nathan van Beusekom #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol277-giscience2023/LIPIcs.GIScience.2023.10/LIPIcs.GIScience.2023.10.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"2D map alignment with region decomposition | Autonomous Robots | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10514-018-9785-7\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Spatial Indexing — Advanced Databases 1.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.siue.edu/~marmcke/docs/cs490/spatialIndexing.html\"},{\"title\":\"Discrete global grid - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_global_grid\"},{\"title\":\"Spatial Index: Grid Systems | Towards Data Science\",\"url\":\"https://towardsdatascience.com/spatial-index-grid-systems-f02880fb147a/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about geographic spatial decomposition, grid placement, and 2D layout:\n\n## Spatial Decomposition and Grid Systems\n\nSpatial decomposition methods are characterized as employing spatial indexing by placing data into uniquely identifiable boxes or cells. In regular decomposition, space is partitioned in a regular or semi-regular manner, and the idea of superimposing a regular pattern of cells over the geometric data has much in common with the raster model of data storage.\n\n## Grid Placement Methods\n\nThe geographical dimensions of the grid (in 2D) are defined by a set of vertical and horizontal partition lines. Spatial subdivision typically involves superimposing a grid of axis-aligned boxes on a scene and recording which geometric primitives overlap which regions.\n\n## Subregion Decomposition\n\nThe region quadtree represents a partition of space in two dimensions by decomposing the region into four equal quadrants, subquadrants, and so on with each leaf node containing data corresponding to a specific subregion. Quadtrees adaptively partition an initial square into four sectors recursively, allowing variable grid resolutions based on local data density.\n\n## Grid Map Layouts\n\nGrid maps schematize each region in the input into a simple shape (a tile), often a rectangle or hexagon, to then subsequently arrange these tiles into a grid, roughly according to geography. The assignment of regions to tiles in a grid is called a layout, and the layout of a grid map is traditionally determined by the geography of the regions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"97fad866-5a8a-492c-9b8e-09f5de56afdd","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:48.482Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"71c12b25-d849-4758-93c3-ab0787c93472","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9a6d8c8d-b292-48c6-9076-2c3443f3fc93","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LtWzjRAP5TigAWyDNhGZYa","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"hybrid CP MIP decomposition CP-SAT Gurobi binding subproblem scheduling\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Programming - Gurobi Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/faqs/constraint-programming/\"},{\"title\":\"Algorithms for Hybrid MILP/CP Models for a Class of Optimization Problems\",\"url\":\"https://cepac.cheme.cmu.edu/pasilectures/grossmann/VipulINFORMSJCMIPCP.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel MIP Solving with Dynamic Task Decomposition Peng Lin # Ñ\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26/LIPIcs.CP.2025.26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"What are the differences between Constraint Programming and MIP? – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/articles/360048197891-What-are-the-differences-between-Constraint-Programming-and-MIP\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding Fundamental Differences Between MIP and CP Solvers in or-tools Context\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/NoGdc_MiXT0\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint for a max, number of overlapping tasks – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/9565554229521-Constraint-for-a-max-number-of-overlapping-tasks\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Model-based Decomposition Algorithms for Complex Routing ...\",\"url\":\"https://tidel.mie.utoronto.ca/pubs/Theses/Zhang_Jiachen_202510_PhD_thesis.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"}]\n\nBased on my web search results, I found relevant information about hybrid CP and MIP approaches, CP-SAT, and Gurobi. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Hybrid CP-MIP Approaches\n\nMany advanced optimization systems integrate constraint programming with solvers like Gurobi to combine strengths. For example, a CP solver might be used to prune infeasible solutions early, while Gurobi is used to optimize the final feasible model using MIP. This hybrid approach can be especially effective in complex decision models that combine logical constraints with numerical optimization.\n\n## CP-SAT Solver\n\nCP-SAT is a CP and a (non-mixed) integer linear programming solver. It has techniques from both world. It is currently the best CP solver and a very good ILP solver.\n\n## Decomposition Approaches for Scheduling\n\nThe aim of these methods is to combine the strength of MILP for proving optimality by using the LP relaxations, and the power of CP for finding feasible solutions by using the specialized propagation algorithms. As an example, they consider a scheduling problem that involves finding a least-cost schedule to process a set of orders using dissimilar parallel machines subject to release and due-date constraints.\n\n## Suitability for Scheduling Problems\n\nCP solvers perform well on, e.g., loosely constrained discrete sequencing problems with disjunctive constraints (for example, job shop scheduling) where the LP relaxation is often weak. Additionally, Constraint programming is commonly applied in scheduling (e.g., employee rosters, manufacturing processes), resource allocation, timetabling, and configuration problems.\n\nThe search results demonstrate that hybrid approaches combining CP and MIP methods, decomposition techniques (like Benders decomposition), and solvers like CP-SAT and Gurobi are well-established methods for tackling complex scheduling optimization problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"6670d040-2ac2-4dc6-9b83-a666ed5dfe5c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:48.717Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9a6d8c8d-b292-48c6-9076-2c3443f3fc93","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4c1421ef-beb5-45fd-834f-6a0f3f16455d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xvu1yomStTrMPcjuxc1dSQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"fix-and-optimize RINS local branching MIP heuristic large neighborhood search Fischetti\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Repairing MIP infeasibility through Local Branching\",\"url\":\"https://www.dei.unipd.it/~fisch/papers/repairing_MIP_infeasibility_through_local_branching.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MIP Heuristics 1\",\"url\":\"https://co-at-work.zib.de/berlin2009/downloads/2009-09-25/2009-09-25-0900-BB-MIP-2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Repairing MIP infeasibility through local branching - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054806001900\"},{\"title\":\"A Pre-processing Aware RINS Based MIP Heuristic | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-38516-2_1\"},{\"title\":\"a primal heuristic based on Feasibility PUmp and SHifting\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2208.00191\"},{\"title\":\"A local branching heuristic for the capacitated fixed-charge network design problem - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054808001585\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search (LNS) for Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP) | Jongmin Mun\",\"url\":\"https://jong-min.org/blog/2026/large-neighborhood-search-milp/\"},{\"title\":\"HEURISTICS IN MIXED INTEGER PROGRAMMING MATTEO FISCHETTI\",\"url\":\"https://homepages.cwi.nl/~dadush/workshop/discrepancy-ip/papers/heuristics-survey-fischetti-lodi-11.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Generalized Relax-and-Fix heuristic - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054822002684\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Repairing MILP infeasibility through local branching\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222570644_Repairing_MILP_infeasibility_through_local_branching\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found comprehensive information about the MIP heuristics you're interested in:\n\n## Key MIP Heuristics Overview\n\nLarge Neighborhood Search (LNS) heuristics for solving complex Mixed-Integer Linear Programs (MILP) explore core concepts, 'destroy and repair' methods, and popular frameworks like RINS and Local Branching.\n\n### Local Branching\n\nIntroduced by Fischetti and Lodi (2003), Local Branching explores the neighborhood of an incumbent solution by adding linear constraints (local branching cuts) to the original problem. Given an incumbent binary solution, a \"local branching constraint\" restricts the search space so that any new solution can differ from the incumbent in at most k binary variables.\n\nThe approach alternates high-level strategic branchings to define solution neighborhoods, and low-level tactical branchings (performed within the MIP solver) to explore them, resulting in a two-level branching strategy aimed at favoring early updatings of the incumbent solution.\n\n### RINS (Relaxation Induced Neighborhood Search)\n\nDesigned by Danna, Rothberg, and Le Pape (2005) and heavily utilized by IBM CPLEX and Gurobi, RINS works by comparing the current best incumbent integer solution against the optimal solution of the continuous LP relaxation at the current node of the branch-and-bound tree. RINS leverages the intuition that variables showing consensus between the continuous relaxation and the integer incumbent are highly likely to maintain those values in an optimal or near-optimal solution.\n\n### Fix-and-Optimize\n\nThe solution can be improved by the generic local search Fix-and-Optimize. This heuristic is related to the relax-and-fix approach but focuses on optimizing fixed portions of the problem.\n\nAll these methods are examples of heuristics like RINS and Local Branching that require zero problem-specific knowledge to implement; they rely strictly on the mathematical structure, and beautifully hybridizes heuristics with exact methods (Matheuristics).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c5403da6-d0fb-413c-950d-78a9a7c353eb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:48.756Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4c1421ef-beb5-45fd-834f-6a0f3f16455d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
