{"parentUuid":"8ad2f83a-a22e-415c-91bd-8a750aefd8aa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af150891e26339789","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_015uURwP956Gozdmmn7LZti6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018jZSKPYPh26r4rUQKYWLhi","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20239","prompt":"Extract details on the multilevel LBBD approach: master problem formulation, subproblem formulation, type of cuts generated, solver used, sizes solved, comparison with monolithic and bottom-up baselines, and any discussion on when multilevel beats single-level Benders."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2834,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2488,"cache_read_input_tokens":27260,"output_tokens":333,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2488},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2834,"output_tokens":333,"cache_read_input_tokens":27260,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2488,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2488,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caorbj4gDf35uiqo55DsY","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"51755468-d15e-4d58-bcc2-48ab5c8d88b6","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:40:54.888Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0e2ecfe0-311e-44e4-b41a-4ef87aead578","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UgQaivitiszRknoLuTPXGM","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition block-diagonal structure detection automatic GCG\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Dantzig–Wolfe decomposition - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dantzig%E2%80%93Wolfe_decomposition\"},{\"title\":\"A data driven Dantzig–Wolfe decomposition framework | Mathematical Programming Computation\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-022-00230-4\"},{\"title\":\"Solving process of GCG | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Solving-process-of-GCG_fig1_221131622\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Generic Dantzig-Wolfe Reformulation of Mixed Integer Programs\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235637963_Generic_Dantzig-Wolfe_Reformulation_of_Mixed_Integer_Programs\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Experience with Hypergraph-Based Methods for Automatic Decomposition in Discrete Optimization | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/267477003_Computational_Experience_with_Hypergraph-Based_Methods_for_Automatic_Decomposition_in_Discrete_Optimization\"},{\"title\":\"1 Outline 2 Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition - Dabeen Lee\",\"url\":\"https://dabeenl.github.io/IE631_lecture22_note.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A data driven Dantzig–Wolfe decomposition framework\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/365052320_A_data_driven_Dantzig-Wolfe_decomposition_framework\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Learning When to Use a Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318136998_Learning_When_to_Use_a_Decomposition\"},{\"title\":\"A computational study of Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228816421_A_computational_study_of_Dantzig-Wolfe_decomposition\"},{\"title\":\"A generic view of Dantzig–Wolfe decomposition in mixed ...\",\"url\":\"https://ise.ncsu.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/9/2018/08/decomposition-A-generic-view-of-Danzig-Wolfe-decomposition-in-mixed-iteger-programming.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition, block-diagonal structure detection, and the GCG solver:\n\n## Overview\n\nDantzig–Wolfe decomposition is an algorithm for solving (mixed integer) linear programming problems by exploiting their structure, originally developed by George Dantzig and Philip Wolfe and initially published in 1960.\n\n## Block-Diagonal Structure\n\nThe main use case for the Dantzig–Wolfe decomposition is when the constraint matrix of the linear program has an 'almost diagonal' structure, where the set of constraints D is usually identified as \"connecting\", \"coupling\", or \"complicating\" constraints. The corresponding sub-problem will be much smaller than the original problem (less constraints and less variables) which means it can be solved faster, and in the case of a diagonal structure the sub-problems are usually less linked.\n\n## Automatic Detection\n\nA key research area involves automating the detection of decomposable structures. The detection (construction) of a matrix structure that is useful for Dantzig-Wolfe reformulation of a MIP can be accomplished by suitably permuting rows and columns. The main blocking factor in designing general purpose solvers is the problem of automatic decomposition of MIPs, that is to produce good MIP decompositions algorithmically, looking only at the algebraic structure of the MIP instance, and researchers propose to employ Dantzig–Wolfe reformulation and machine learning methods to obtain a fully data driven automatic decomposition framework.\n\n## GCG Solver\n\nGenerally, the block structures are not readily apparent in raw form, and to identify and exploit these block structures, a structure detector is implemented in the Generic Column Generation (GCG) solver. GCG's detection loop for structured matrices and the coordination of pricing routines for Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition has been significantly revised for greater flexibility.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"706ee0ab-1b8c-4993-860b-445e02f41c2a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:00.281Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e2ecfe0-311e-44e4-b41a-4ef87aead578","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8ad2f83a-a22e-415c-91bd-8a750aefd8aa","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YCsrxAwZXxuHiK4LbgBaTh","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"approximate dynamic programming geometric packing facility layout decomposition\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Dynamic Site Layout Planning Using Approximate Dynamic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/239386752_Dynamic_Site_Layout_Planning_Using_Approximate_Dynamic_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"Solution procedures for the dynamic facility layout problem | Annals of Operations Research\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1018904806854\"},{\"title\":\"Approximation Algorithms for Two-Dimensional Geometric Packing Problems\",\"url\":\"https://people.idsia.ch/~grandoni/PhD_Theses/ThesisWaldoGalvez.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solution Techniques for Two-dimensional Cutting and Packing\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0377221720306111\"},{\"title\":\"Packing problems on generalised regular grid: Levels of abstraction using integer linear programming - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1524070323000358\"},{\"title\":\"Hierarchical Rectangle Packing Solved by Multi-Level Recursive Logic-based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20239\"},{\"title\":\"Bin Packing Problem - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/bin-packing-problem\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solution Techniques for Two-dimensional Cutting and Packing\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.12619\"},{\"title\":\"Packing optimization of practical systems using a dynamic acceleration methodology | Journal of Engineering and Applied Science | Full Text\",\"url\":\"https://jeas.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s44147-024-00426-6\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Using Decomposition Techniques and Constraint Programming for Solving the Two-Dimensional Bin-Packing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220669044_Using_Decomposition_Techniques_and_Constraint_Programming_for_Solving_the_Two-Dimensional_Bin-Packing_Problem\"}]\n\nI found several relevant sources about approximate dynamic programming, geometric packing, facility layout, and decomposition techniques. Here's what the search results show:\n\n## Key Topics Found:\n\n**Approximate Dynamic Programming for Facility Layout:**\nAn approximate dynamic programming model has been developed that is capable of searching for and identifying global optimal dynamic site layout plans by applying the concepts of approximate dynamic programming to estimate the future effects of layout decisions in early stages on future decisions in later stages.\n\n**Dynamic Programming for Geometric Packing:**\nState space relaxation, originally developed for routing problems, modifies the state space of a dynamic programming recursion so that its optimal solution is a valid bound for the original problem. Researchers have restricted the 2D bin packing problem to two-stage guillotine patterns that can be solved through dynamic programming, using a branch-and-price framework.\n\n**Decomposition Techniques:**\nDecomposition approaches use the Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition where the master problem deals with production constraints while the subproblem deals with packing of rectangles into a single bin, which is solved as a constraint-satisfaction problem. Decomposition has been applied to solve strip packing problems and 2D bin packing problems with an ILP model based on classical arc-flow formulation.\n\n**Hierarchical Packing and Facility Layout:**\nThe two-dimensional hierarchical rectangle packing problem, motivated by applications in analog integrated circuit layout, facility layout, and logistics, involves inherently hierarchical packing where each item is either a rectangle or a block occurrence whose dimensions are a solution of another packing problem.\n\nThese research areas intersect in addressing complex optimization problems that require combining approximate methods with decomposition strategies to handle practical applications in facility layout and geometric packing.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"739ecb2d-eb17-4aea-a8b2-e0f753b82e02","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:00.316Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8ad2f83a-a22e-415c-91bd-8a750aefd8aa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"51755468-d15e-4d58-bcc2-48ab5c8d88b6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018jZSKPYPh26r4rUQKYWLhi","type":"tool_result","content":"# Multi-Level Logic-based Benders Decomposition for Hierarchical Rectangle Packing\n\n## Master Problem Formulation\n\nThe master problem at each block B^i minimizes the block's height while respecting a maximum width constraint from the parent. The formulation includes:\n\n- The partial MILP model for B^i (equations 5-24)\n- A width constraint from parent: W^i ≤ W^i_PLAN\n- Area lower bounds for child blocks: \"W^i' · H^i' ≥ LB_area(B^i'), ∀i' ∈ C^i\"\n- Dynamically generated feasibility cuts\n\n## Subproblem Formulation\n\nChild blocks are solved recursively with their own master problems, receiving maximum width constraints from parents. The key constraint linking parent and child is:\n\n\"w_I_k = opt_σ(I_k), h_I_k = opt_σ(I_k)\" \n\nThis ensures block occurrence dimensions match their referenced block's solution.\n\n## Cut Generation\n\nTwo types of cuts eliminate infeasible dimension pairs:\n\n1. **Feasibility cuts** (equation 54): \"W^i' ≤ W^i'_PLAN ⟹ H^i' ≥ H^i'_ACT\" when a child cannot achieve the parent's suggested width\n2. **Strengthening cuts** (equation 61): \"W^i' < W^i'_RIGHT ⟹ H^i' ≥ H^i'_LEFT\" from fine-tuning analysis\n\nThese cuts progressively reduce the feasible region, as illustrated in Figure 2(b).\n\n## Solver Selection\n\nCP Optimizer was chosen over Gurobi MILP. Table 2 demonstrates: \"M-CP performs best for the mid-sized instances with 20-55 rectangles\" with superior gap percentages across benchmark sets.\n\n## Problem Sizes Solved\n\nThe paper reports results on instances with:\n- Up to 7 hierarchy levels\n- 80 items per block maximum  \n- 402 total objects in largest instances\n- Computation times from 10 to 240 minutes\n\n## Performance Comparison\n\n**Two-level instances (Table 3):** LBBD consistently outperforms monolithic methods. For L2-L, LBBD_B achieves 2.71% W+H gap versus M-CP's 8.85%.\n\n**Multi-level instances:** The paper states LBBD \"outperforms both monolithic formulations and the Bottom-Up method in terms of solution quality and scalability.\"\n\n## When Multi-Level LBBD Wins\n\nThe decomposition excels because:\n\n1. It dynamically refines dimension constraints rather than requiring manual candidate selection\n2. Bottom-Up wastes computation generating unused variants; LBBD focuses on promising dimensions\n3. The recursive structure directly mirrors the problem hierarchy, enabling effective constraint propagation through cuts\n\nThe authors note Bottom-Up's weakness: \"it cannot reason which packing variants would be useful at the top node\" requiring \"multiple variants to ensure one actually works well.\""}]},"uuid":"5cccbaa3-7c95-472f-b3db-041328770855","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:05.600Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"51755468-d15e-4d58-bcc2-48ab5c8d88b6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"5cccbaa3-7c95-472f-b3db-041328770855","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af150891e26339789","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016fK4EnD4Lt7Ndn8emWhDYg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough material now. Let me do one more focused search on the specific overlap of column generation with placement-with-routing problems."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7267,"cache_read_input_tokens":29748,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7267,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorcwxbNKrZhmy6nBovJ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"03fab09f-cd12-4058-ad99-8be88bfed945","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:08.642Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"03fab09f-cd12-4058-ad99-8be88bfed945","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af150891e26339789","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016fK4EnD4Lt7Ndn8emWhDYg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013X8ptPZ6U618sdhDgn9Xfu","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"branch-price-and-cut facility placement with routing coupled subproblems exact"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7267,"cache_read_input_tokens":29748,"output_tokens":128,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7267},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":128,"cache_read_input_tokens":29748,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7267,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7267,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorcwxbNKrZhmy6nBovJ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6383d205-591e-42c6-b3c1-2d31044da98a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:09.401Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6383d205-591e-42c6-b3c1-2d31044da98a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"af150891e26339789","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013X8ptPZ6U618sdhDgn9Xfu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"branch-price-and-cut facility placement with routing coupled subproblems exact\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"﻿Partial dominance in branch-price-and-cut algorithms for vehicle routing and scheduling problems with a single-segment tradeoff | OR Spectrum | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00291-024-00766-y\"},{\"title\":\"A branch-and-price-and-cut algorithm for the vehicle routing problem with load-dependent drones - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0191261523000383\"},{\"title\":\"Exact branch-price-and-cut algorithms for vehicle routing | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326096337_Exact_branch-price-and-cut_algorithms_for_vehicle_routing\"},{\"title\":\"A branch-and-price algorithm for a green location routing problem with multi-type charging infrastructure | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/356554271_A_branch-and-price_algorithm_for_a_green_location_routing_problem_with_multi-type_charging_infrastructure\"},{\"title\":\"A branch-and-cut-and-price algorithm for the multi-depot heterogeneous vehicle routing problem with time windows - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0968090X10001178\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Robust Branch-and-Cut-and-Price for the Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220590043_Robust_Branch-and-Cut-and-Price_for_the_Capacitated_Vehicle_Routing_Problem\"},{\"title\":\"Branch-Cut-and-Price for the Robust Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem with Knapsack Uncertainty | Operations Research\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/abs/10.1287/opre.2020.2035\"},{\"title\":\"A branch-price-and-cut algorithm for the vehicle routing problem with release and due dates - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1366554520308103\"},{\"title\":\"Quantum Subroutines in Branch-Price-and-Cut for Vehicle Routing\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.15665v1\"},{\"title\":\"Robust Branch-and-Cut-and-Price for the Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://renatowerneck.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/flpruw04-cvrp-ipco.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant research on branch-price-and-cut algorithms, though the specific combination of \"facility placement with routing coupled subproblems\" is not directly featured as a primary topic. However, the results show related work in this area:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Branch-Price-and-Cut Methodology**: A branch-and-price algorithm is a branch-and-bound algorithm in which lower bounds are computed by column generation, solving a restricted master problem and one or several pricing subproblems, with cutting planes added to strengthen linear relaxations.\n\n**Related Applications with Coupled Subproblems**:\n\n1. **Vehicle Routing with Facility Elements**: Research on vehicle routing problems with docking hubs introduces facilities to extend service coverage, with branch-and-price-and-cut algorithms developed based on Danzig-Wolfe decomposition.\n\n2. **Location-Routing Problems**: Studies have determined the location of depots with energy-restore facilities and routing decisions simultaneously, including charging station allocation problems studied along with routing decisions.\n\n3. **Facility Location in General**: Facility location has been identified as a remarkable application where pricing subproblems are integer programs, with column generation applied historically to facility location problems.\n\nThe search results indicate that branch-price-and-cut algorithms with coupled subproblems for facility placement and routing decisions exist in the literature, but a more specific search might be needed to find the exact paper or framework you're looking for.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"65fa1788-bda7-43a7-90b2-34c4c6979a2a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:16.282Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6383d205-591e-42c6-b3c1-2d31044da98a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"65fa1788-bda7-43a7-90b2-34c4c6979a2a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af150891e26339789","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_011iFBxduQZoL6cWcFhtUTNC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough material across all 9 topics. Here is the synthesis.\n\n---\n\n# 大规模组合优化分解方法调研：能否替代/补充我们的 LBBD\n\n## 1. Column Generation / Branch-and-Price (B&P)\n\n把\"一个完整可行的 line（一组设施 + 端口绑定 + 路径）\"展成一个 column，master 用 set-covering/set-partitioning LP 选 column 组合，pricing subproblem 找负 reduced cost 的新 column。**适用条件：**block-angular 结构 + master 容易写成 covering/partitioning。**我们项目可用度：**中等。266 个 mandatory instances 都是必放的（partitioning 而非 covering），column 空间是\"某条 line 的所有合法放法\"，几何上 column 数爆炸。pricing subproblem 本身就是一个 mini-CP-SAT。**工程量：**重——需重写 master、做稳定化（Wentges/dual stabilization）、设计 branching rule（Ryan-Foster 之类）。3-6 人月起步。经典案例：cutting stock (Gilmore-Gomory)、crew scheduling、2D level strip packing ([Lodi et al.](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10288-007-0051-7))。\n\n## 2. Dantzig-Wolfe Decomposition (DWD)\n\n理论上等价于 column generation 的母版方法，把 block-angular MIP 拆成 master + 多个独立 subproblem。**适用条件：**约束矩阵在适当行列重排后是 block-diagonal + coupling。**我们项目可用度：**低。我们 placement、binding、routing **共享同一套网格**，coupling 太密，难以剥出干净 block。**工程量：**可以用 [GCG (SCIP)](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235637963_Generic_Dantzig-Wolfe_Reformulation_of_Mixed_Integer_Programs) 自动检测尝试，零成本试一下；如果 GCG 检测不出干净结构，就放弃。\n\n## 3. Hierarchical / Multi-Level Benders\n\n经典 Benders 是两层；多层是 master -> sub1（自身又是 master） -> sub2 ...，每层动态生成传给上层的 cut。**与我们高度相关：**[arXiv 2512.20239 (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.20239) \"Hierarchical Rectangle Packing by Multi-Level LBBD\" 用 CP Optimizer 做 master，**dynamic refinement of dimension constraints**，在 7 层、80 items/block 上击败 monolithic 和 bottom-up。**我们项目可用度：**高——我们已经事实上四层（master/binding/routing/flow），但目前是 **flat pipeline**（一层 fail 整个回到 master）。改成 **recursive refinement**（routing 失败时让 binding 知道哪些 port 组合是几何不可达的，binding 失败时让 master 知道哪些 placement 等价类是不可绑的）会显著减少 master 重解次数。**工程量：**中。要给每层定义 cut 提取接口和\"上层等价类\"的概念。Hooker 系列经典参考 [Planning and Scheduling by LBBD](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/opre.1060.0371)。\n\n## 4. Combinatorial Benders Cuts (Codato-Fischetti 2006)\n\n[Codato & Fischetti 2006](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/abs/10.1287/opre.1060.0286) 针对纯整数 master + 含 big-M 的 sub，cut 不依赖 big-M 而是从 **minimal infeasible subsystems (MIS)** 中提取纯组合不等式。**我们项目可用度：**很高。我们 routing/binding 失败时目前生成的 no-good cut（\"这组 placement 全禁\"）非常弱。换成 \"这组 placement 中的某个最小不可路由子集禁\"会让 master 的 LP 松弛紧得多。**工程量：**小到中。需要在 routing/binding sub fail 时跑一次 MIS 提取（IIS in Gurobi，或手写 deletion filter）。**强烈推荐做。**\n\n## 5. Lagrangian Relaxation / Dual Decomposition\n\n把\"placement 与 routing 共享格子\"这条 coupling 约束 dualize 进目标，sub 之间用 multiplier 协调。[ADMM 风格](https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/transe/v192y2024ics1366554524003478.html) 也是同类思想。**我们项目可用度：**低（对精确证明而言）。Lagrangian 给 dual bound，但要 close gap 仍要回到 BB；对 lex 目标 `max(area, min_side)` 的 multiplier 调度也尴尬。**适合做加速 lower bound** 而不是替代 LBBD。**工程量：**中等。Lagrangian survey: [arXiv 2301.00573](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.00573)。\n\n## 6. Fix-and-Optimize / RINS / Local Branching\n\n[Fischetti & Lodi 的 Local Branching](https://www.dei.unipd.it/~fisch/papers/repairing_MIP_infeasibility_through_local_branching.pdf)、[RINS (Danna et al. 2005)](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-38516-2_1) 都是 MIP matheuristic。给定 incumbent，加一个 Hamming 半径 ≤ k 的约束在邻域搜索。**我们项目可用度：**中——**作为暖启动 + frontier improver** 很值得加。我们已有不少 incumbent（campaign checkpoint），fix 90% placement、re-solve 10%，远比从头跑快。**违反 AI Safety Contract 吗？**不违反——这是 deterministic MIP heuristic，不是 AI。**工程量：**小。CP-SAT 直接支持 hint + assumption，加 Hamming-radius assumption 即可。\n\n## 7. Approximate Dynamic Programming (ADP / ALP)\n\n把放置过程建成顺序决策（按某种 order 一个个放设施），用 LP 近似 value function。**我们项目可用度：**低。我们目标 `max_lex(area, min_side)` 高度非可加，ADP 的\"future value\"概念弱。**工程量：**大且 ROI 不明。可作为 **frontier ordering 的 AI heuristic**（顺便满足 AI Safety Contract 中\"order_only\"），但不替代 exact path。\n\n## 8. 2D 几何分解（地理分块）\n\n把 70×70 切成 4×35×35 或 quadtree-like 分区，分别求解再拼接。**我们项目可用度：**低（对精确性而言）。70×70 的 ghost rectangle 可能横跨切线，266 mandatory facility 也跨区分布，**接缝处的最优解会被切掉**。**适合：exploratory 模式 / warm start**——快速给一个下界用于 master 剪枝。**工程量：**中。如果未来要扩到 valley4_infra_outpost 等多 base 同跑，地理分解会很有用。\n\n## 9. Hybrid CP/MIP Decomposition\n\nMaster 用 CP-SAT（我们已经是），sub 用 Gurobi/CPLEX MIP，专门解 binding（assignment 类）这种 MIP 强项问题。Hooker 在 [Planning and Scheduling LBBD](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/opre.1060.0371) 里就是这个范式。**我们项目可用度：**高。我们 binding subproblem 是个 assignment LP/MIP，CP-SAT 在这上面不见得最快。**工程量：**中。引入 Gurobi/HiGHS 依赖，写一个 binding 的 MIP 版做 A/B benchmark。如果 Gurobi 在 binding 上快 3 倍以上，切过去的 ROI 很高。HiGHS 是开源替代。\n\n---\n\n## 我们项目的分解结构推荐\n\n**保留 LBBD 主干，但做三处增强（按 ROI 排序）：**\n\n1. **【最高 ROI、最低风险】Combinatorial Benders Cuts (Codato-Fischetti)：**给现有 routing/binding sub 加 MIS 提取，no-good cut 升级成最小不可行子集 cut。预期 master LP 松弛大幅收紧、Benders 迭代次数下降一个量级。\n\n2. **【高 ROI、中风险】Multi-Level / Hierarchical LBBD（参 arXiv 2512.20239）：**把当前\"任何 sub fail 就回到 master\"的 flat pipeline 改成\"binding fail 留在 binding 层尝试 alt port 组合，routing fail 留在 routing 层尝试 reroute\"。每层动态精化。这与我们已有的 master+binding+routing+flow 四层结构天然契合，工程上是把 fall-through 改成 nested fix-point。\n\n3. **【中 ROI、低风险】RINS / Local Branching warm-start：**campaign checkpoint 复活时，加 Hamming-半径 assumption，让 CP-SAT 在 incumbent 邻域而不是全空间搜索。直接利用现有 hint 机制，不破坏 exactness（半径放开后仍是完整空间）。\n\n**不推荐切换的：**纯 Column Generation / DWD（结构不匹配，工程量大）、ADP（目标函数不适合）、地理分块（破坏精确性）。\n\n**值得做 A/B 评估的：**Hybrid CP/MIP——把 binding 子问题在 HiGHS/Gurobi 上对比一下；如果 MIP 有 3x+ 加速且 cut 接口能保留，再切。\n\n**与 AI Safety Contract 的关系：**以上 1/2/3 都是**确定性数学方法**，不属于 AI sidecar，不影响 contract。AI 可以继续做 ordering/分类/解释，但分解结构本身的改造走 deterministic lane。\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Branch-and-Price: Column Generation for Solving Huge Integer Programs (Barnhart et al.)](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/opre.46.3.316)\n- [Optimizing with Column Generation (open book)](https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Book_on_Column_Generation___Part_I.pdf)\n- [Selected Topics in Column Generation (Lübbecke)](https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2002/12/580.pdf)\n- [Branch-and-price for 2D level strip packing](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10288-007-0051-7)\n- [Logic-Based Benders Decomposition: Theory and Applications (Hooker book, Springer)](https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-031-45039-6)\n- [Hooker — LBBD for Large-scale Optimization (chapter PDF)](https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/LBBDbookChapter.pdf)\n- [Planning and Scheduling by LBBD (Hooker, OR 2007)](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/opre.1060.0371)\n- [Hierarchical Rectangle Packing by Multi-Level LBBD (arXiv 2512.20239)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.20239)\n- [Codato & Fischetti — Combinatorial Benders' Cuts (OR 2006)](https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/abs/10.1287/opre.1060.0286)\n- [Rahmaniani — Benders Decomposition: A Literature Review](https://www.cirrelt.ca/documentstravail/cirrelt-2016-30.pdf)\n- [Survey on Lagrangian Relaxation for MILP (arXiv 2301.00573)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.00573)\n- [Fischetti & Lodi — Repairing MIP infeasibility through Local Branching](https://www.dei.unipd.it/~fisch/papers/repairing_MIP_infeasibility_through_local_branching.pdf)\n- [Fischetti & Lodi — Heuristics in MIP (survey)](https://homepages.cwi.nl/~dadush/workshop/discrepancy-ip/papers/heuristics-survey-fischetti-lodi-11.pdf)\n- [RINS preprocessing-aware variant](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-38516-2_1)\n- [Dantzig-Wolfe — generic reformulation (GCG)](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/235637963_Generic_Dantzig-Wolfe_Reformulation_of_Mixed_Integer_Programs)\n- [Data-driven Dantzig-Wolfe decomposition framework](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-022-00230-4)\n- [Hybrid MILP/CP algorithms (Grossmann group)](https://cepac.cheme.cmu.edu/pasilectures/grossmann/VipulINFORMSJCMIPCP.pdf)\n- [Exact Solution Techniques for 2D Cutting and Packing (arXiv 2004.12619)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.12619)\n- [Dynamic Site Layout Planning by ADP](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/239386752_Dynamic_Site_Layout_Planning_Using_Approximate_Dynamic_Programming)\n- [Quadtree (spatial decomposition primer)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadtree)"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1606,"cache_read_input_tokens":37015,"output_tokens":4767,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1606},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4767,"cache_read_input_tokens":37015,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1606,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaordjLbfMezQrwjqmqU2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fdc62928-118c-43cf-aef1-73c80e954260","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:41.081Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
